{"route_a": {"warrants_alone": "partly", "why": "Route A pairs a qualitative EBSD colour-pattern observation (uniform colour in lenticular/{10-12} twin bands vs. continuous shading in wavy bands) with a quantitative SAED reading (c-axis direction varying strongly and non-monotonically, ~60/180/40/10 deg, across four points A-D on a run of wavy/kink bands). The SAED data is real diffraction evidence that orientation genuinely changes point-to-point within the wavy band population, which is stronger than an image-contrast argument alone. But its own stated limits are substantial: only four SAED points (can't rule out discrete stepped reorientation vs. smooth continuous rotation), no SAED was taken on the twin bands for a matched comparison, and the SAED angles are referenced to a fixed horizontal direction rather than to the matrix, so it isn't on a calibrated common scale with the EBSD panel. The twin-vs-kink contrast itself rests on EBSD colour uniformity alone, not on any SAED reading. So Route A supports the wavy-band-varies / twin-band-uniform conclusion but does not fully warrant it unaided."},
"route_b": {"warrants_alone": "partly", "why": "Route B links OM band morphology (straight {10-12}/{01-12} traces plus an asymmetric optical contrast vs. wavy bands) to EBSD colour behaviour (uniform vs. continuously shading) for the same specimens. This shows the two band populations distinguished optically are the same populations distinguished by EBSD colour pattern, which is a genuine cross-modality correspondence. But per its own limits, the pairing is purely qualitative (colour/contrast, not a measured misorientation magnitude), covers only two specimen orientations, links the two image types by spatial co-registration of band type rather than by measuring the same physical quantity, and compares two different kinds of contrast (optical topographic/diffraction contrast vs. crystallographic orientation colour coding) whose agreement in classifying bands does not prove they track the same underlying variable. So it establishes a correlation between morphology and orientation-map signature, but not, by itself, that the wavy bands' internal orientation is truly varying in a crystallographically meaningful way (that comes from Route A's SAED, not from Route B)."},
"agree": "partly", "why_agree": "The two routes are consistent in their conclusions (wavy/kink bands: internally varying orientation; lenticular/twin bands: uniform orientation) and their limits are compatible rather than contradictory. However, the prompt's framing of 'disjoint evidence' is not quite accurate: both routes cite the identical EBSD orientation-map observation (uniform colour in lenticular bands vs. continuous shading in wavy bands, with the [01-10] twin/kink coexistence) as one of their two evidence legs. So the routes are not two independent confirmations from scratch; they share a common leg (EBSD) and differ only in their second leg (Route A: SAED; Route B: OM). What each route adds beyond the shared EBSD leg is complementary -- SAED gives a real crystallographic angle spread, OM gives an optical/morphological correlate -- and neither leg's limits undercut the other's claims, so the routes are compatible. But because they overlap in evidence, combining them yields less independent corroboration than the two-routes framing suggests.", "combined_strength": "associative", "caution": "A reader should not treat the two routes as fully independent triangulation, since both rely on the same EBSD orientation-map observation as one of their two evidentiary legs -- only the second leg differs (SAED vs. OM). The combination should not be read as establishing a quantified misorientation magnitude (SAED gives only four discrete angle readings on wavy bands, not a systematic profile, and was never applied to twin bands for comparison), nor as establishing a causal/mechanistic account of why kink bands show continuously varying orientation while twin bands do not (dislocation-mediated rotation vs. discrete twinning is implied by the pattern of observations but not demonstrated). The agreement of two partly-warranted routes that share a common evidentiary leg does not amount to strong, discriminating proof; it is best read as reinforcing correlational/associative support for the claim, not a validated mechanism."}